        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Planarity and Graph Structure",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "This is the second escape hatch from `07`'s hardness, and the more profound one:\ninstead of relaxing to linear algebra (`08`), *exploit the graph's structure*.\nPlanar graphs (drawable without crossings) and bounded-treewidth graphs (\"tree-\nlike\" graphs) admit polynomial or even linear algorithms for problems that are\nNP-hard in general. The crowning theory — Robertson-Seymour graph minors — is one\nof the deepest results in combinatorics, and it explains *why* structure defeats\nhardness in a uniform way."
    },
    {
      "id": "09-PLANARITY-AND-STRUCTURE.md#002",
